# <%= projectName %>

An [AWS CDK](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cdk/v2/guide/home.html) app. Each
stage in `src/config.ts` (<%= stages.map((stage) => `\`${stage.name}\``).join(', ') %>) is a CDK
`Stage`, so its stacks deploy as `<stage>-<Stack>`.

## Setup

1. Set the AWS account ID for each stage in `src/config.ts`.
1. Configure AWS credentials, for example with a named profile in
   `~/.aws/config`.
1. Bootstrap each account and region once:

   ```sh
   nx bootstrap <%= projectName %> -- --profile <profile>
   ```

Lambda functions are bundled locally with esbuild, so Docker isn't needed.

## Commands

Pass AWS CLI options after `--`, e.g. `-- --profile <profile>`, or set
`AWS_PROFILE`.

| Command                                   | What it does                                                    |
| ----------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `nx synth <%= projectName %>`             | Typecheck, then synthesize every stage into `cdk.out`.          |
| `nx diff <%= projectName %> -c <stage>`   | Show what a deploy of the stage would change.                   |
| `nx deploy <%= projectName %> -c <stage>` | Deploy every stack in the stage. Defaults to `<%= stages[0].name %>`. |
| `nx cdk <%= projectName %> -- <args>`     | Run any CDK CLI command, e.g. `-- deploy '<%= stages[0].name %>/Example'`.   |

Non-production deploys skip the approval prompt. Production deploys keep it, so
the CLI asks before applying changes that widen IAM permissions or security
group rules.

## Adding a stack

```sh
nx g @wuguishifu/nx-aws-cdk:stack <name> --project=<%= projectName %>
```

This creates `src/stacks/<name>/` and adds the stack to `src/app-stage.ts`.
Write Lambda functions with `NodeFunction` from `src/constructs/node-function.ts`
to get the shared defaults: Node.js 24 on arm64, JSON logs, a log group with a
retention period, and source maps.

## Secrets

Don't put secrets in Lambda environment variables. They're visible in the
synthesized template and the Lambda console. Store them in Secrets Manager,
grant the function read access (`secret.grantRead(fn)`), and pass only the
secret's name or ARN in the environment.
